    "gold_solution": "```python\nfrom gurobipy import *\n\ndef solve_problem():\n    m = Model(\"Disaster_Response_Logistics\")\n    m.setParam(\"TimeLimit\", 300)  # Set time limit to 300 seconds\n\n    # Data\n    depots = list(range(3))  # D0..D2\n    units = list(range(3))  # U0..U2\n    supplies = list(range(3))  # S0..S2\n    days = list(range(1))  # Day0..Day0\n\n    demands = [[[45.54], [18.11], [45.42]], [[23.74], [47.11], [34.27]], [[43.66], [14.99], [16.49]]]\n    depot_supplies = [[[53.92], [28.32], [43.04]], [[43.5], [29.61], [35.13]], [[49.92], [33.95], [46.01]]]\n    transport_costs = [[1.41, 3.89, 1.07], [2.04, 4.22, 1.91], [1.41, 1.92, 1.14]]\n    risk_costs = [[155.74, 53.93, 111.82], [182.33, 159.66, 147.22], [120.19, 154.96, 178.44]]\n    supply_volumes = [1.69, 1.35, 1.72]\n    penalty_costs = [94.45, 80.74, 93.9]\n    vehicle_cost = 10.0\n    vehicle_capacity = 100.0\n    total_vehicles = 20\n\n    # Decision variables\n    # Flow_D{d}_U{u}_S{s}_Day{t}: Amount of supply s from depot d to unit u on day t\n    flows = {}\n    for t in days:\n        for d in depots:\n            for u in units:\n                for s in supplies:\n                    flows[(d, u, s, t)] = m.addVar(lb=0.0, vtype=GRB.CONTINUOUS, name=f\"Flow_D{d}_U{u}_S{s}_Day{t}\")\n\n    # Slack_U{u}_S{s}_Day{t}: Shortage of supply s at unit u on day t\n    slacks = {}\n    for t in days:\n        for u in units:\n            for s in supplies:\n                slacks[(u, s, t)] = m.addVar(lb=0.0, vtype=GRB.CONTINUOUS, name=f\"Slack_U{u}_S{s}_Day{t}\")\n\n    # Veh_D{d}_U{u}_Day{t}: Number of vehicles on route d->u on day t\n    vehicles = {}\n    for t in days:\n        for d in depots:\n            for u in units:\n                vehicles[(d, u, t)] = m.addVar(lb=0.0, vtype=GRB.INTEGER, name=f\"Veh_D{d}_U{u}_Day{t}\")\n\n    # Route_D{d}_U{u}_Day{t}: Route d->u is active on day t (binary)\n    routes = {}\n    for t in days:\n        for d in depots:\n            for u in units:\n                routes[(d, u, t)] = m.addVar(vtype=GRB.BINARY, name=f\"Route_D{d}_U{u}_Day{t}\")\n\n    # Objective: Minimize total cost (transport + penalty + vehicle + risk)\n    obj = quicksum(\n        transport_costs[d][u] * flows[(d, u, s, t)]\n        for t in days for d in depots for u in units for s in supplies\n    )\n    obj += quicksum(\n        penalty_costs[s] * slacks[(u, s, t)]\n        for t in days for u in units for s in supplies\n    )\n    obj += quicksum(\n        vehicle_cost * vehicles[(d, u, t)]\n        for t in days for d in depots for u in units\n    )\n    obj += quicksum(\n        risk_costs[d][u] * routes[(d, u, t)]\n        for t in days for d in depots for u in units\n    )\n    m.setObjective(obj, GRB.MINIMIZE)\n\n    # Depot capacity constraints: Total outflow <= supply\n    for t in days:\n        for d in depots:\n            for s in supplies:\n                m.addConstr(\n                    quicksum(flows[(d, u, s, t)] for u in units) <= depot_supplies[d][s][t],\n                    name=f\"DepotCap_D{d}_S{s}_Day{t}\"\n                )\n\n    # Demand constraints: Inflow + slack >= demand\n    for t in days:\n        for u in units:\n            for s in supplies:\n                m.addConstr(\n                    quicksum(flows[(d, u, s, t)] for d in depots) + slacks[(u, s, t)] >= demands[u][s][t],\n                    name=f\"Demand_U{u}_S{s}_Day{t}\"\n                )\n\n    # Vehicle capacity constraints: Total volume <= vehicles * capacity\n    for t in days:\n        for d in depots:\n            for u in units:\n                m.addConstr(\n                    quicksum(supply_volumes[s] * flows[(d, u, s, t)] for s in supplies) <= vehicle_capacity * vehicles[(d, u, t)],\n                    name=f\"VehCap_D{d}_U{u}_Day{t}\"\n                )\n\n    # Route activation constraints: vehicles <= total_vehicles * route\n    for t in days:\n        for d in depots:\n            for u in units:\n                m.addConstr(\n                    vehicles[(d, u, t)] <= total_vehicles * routes[(d, u, t)],\n                    name=f\"RouteAct_D{d}_U{u}_Day{t}\"\n                )\n\n    # Fleet size constraints: Total vehicles <= total_vehicles\n    for t in days:\n        m.addConstr(\n            quicksum(vehicles[(d, u, t)] for d in depots for u in units) <= total_vehicles,\n            name=f\"FleetSize_Day{t}\"\n        )\n\n    m.optimize()\n    return m\n```",
    "LLM_description": "Situation:\nA severe regional disaster has disrupted surface lines of communication. Supply Depot Able (airfield), Supply Depot Bravo (seaport), and Logistics Hub Charlie (railhead) are operational with limited inventories and handling capacity. Relief Battalion Bravo at Forward Operating Base Alpha, Field Hospital Echo in the urban core, and Displacement Camp Delta along the coastal belt require immediate resupply of critical commodities. Key corridors traverse damaged bridges, unstable slopes, and areas requiring security escort. Convoy movements must be consolidated onto cleared routes and timed to available force protection windows. Some commodities are bulky or heavy, constraining payloads per vehicle and requiring careful load planning.\n\nMission:\nDeliver priority life-sustaining supplies from depots to supported units within the current operational period while minimizing total operational cost. Cost elements include per-unit transport costs, shortage penalties for any unmet demand, per-vehicle deployment costs, and fixed route security risk costs that are incurred whenever a route is activated.\n\nExecution/Concept:\n- Source constraints: Each depot may issue only up to on-hand stock per commodity and period. No cross-loading between depots is assumed this period.\n- Demand fulfillment: Each supported unit has commodity-specific minimum requirements. Controlled shortfalls are permitted but incur penalties; these will be accepted only when the combined transport, vehicle, and route activation costs exceed the shortage penalty.\n- Vehicle capacity: Loads are governed by commodity volume/weight factors. The total loaded volume per depot-to-unit movement must not exceed vehicle capacity multiplied by the number of assigned vehicles.\n- Route activation and risk: Activating a route\u2014defined as dispatching any vehicles on a specific depot-to-unit corridor\u2014triggers a fixed Risk Cost representing escorts, route clearance, and quick-reaction force readiness. This fixed cost is charged once per activated route per period, regardless of how many vehicles traverse it. Additional per-unit transport costs still apply.\n- Fleet allocation: A limited fleet is available across all corridors. Vehicles may only operate on routes that have been activated. The aggregate number of deployed vehicles across all routes cannot exceed the fleet available this period.\n- Cost-minimizing approach: Prioritize flows that satisfy the highest-penalty commodities and destinations first, consolidate shipments onto the fewest feasible activated routes to amortize the fixed risk cost, and draw from the most cost-efficient depots without exceeding stock limits. Activate additional routes only when the marginal benefit of reduced shortages or lower transport costs outweighs the fixed risk and vehicle deployment costs.\n- Security and movement control: Movement Control will schedule convoys to align with engineer clearance and escort availability. Receiving units will provide offload teams and secure reception areas. If hazards or access degrade further, shipments will be re-routed to pre-identified alternates using the same activation cost logic.\n\nAnnex A - Logistics Data:\nUnit Demands (Units x Supplies):\nUnit\\Supply | S 0 | S 1 | S 2\n-----------------------------\nU 0        |  45.54 |  18.11 |  45.42\nU 1        |  23.74 |  47.11 |  34.27\nU 2        |  43.66 |  14.99 |  16.49\nDepot Supplies (Depots x Supplies):\nDepot\\Supply | S 0 | S 1 | S 2\n------------------------------\nD 0        |  53.92 |  28.32 |  43.04\nD 1        |  43.50 |  29.61 |  35.13\nD 2        |  49.92 |  33.95 |  46.01\nTransport Costs (Depot x Unit):\nDepot\\Unit | U 0 | U 1 | U 2\n----------------------------\nD 0        |   1.41 |   3.89 |   1.07\nD 1        |   2.04 |   4.22 |   1.91\nD 2        |   1.41 |   1.92 |   1.14\nRisk Costs (Depot x Unit):\nDepot\\Unit | U 0 | U 1 | U 2\n----------------------------\nD 0        | 155.74 |  53.93 | 111.82\nD 1        | 182.33 | 159.66 | 147.22\nD 2        | 120.19 | 154.96 | 178.44\nPenalty Costs per Unit Shortage: [Supply S0: 94.45, Supply S1: 80.74, Supply S2: 93.9]\nVolume per Supply Unit: [Supply S0: 1.69, Supply S1: 1.35, Supply S2: 1.72]\nFleet Parameters: Cost 10.0, Capacity 100.0, Total 20",
    "LLM_description_template": "Situation:\nA severe regional disaster has disrupted surface lines of communication. Supply Depot Able (airfield), Supply Depot Bravo (seaport), and Logistics Hub Charlie (railhead) are operational with limited inventories and handling capacity. Relief Battalion Bravo at Forward Operating Base Alpha, Field Hospital Echo in the urban core, and Displacement Camp Delta along the coastal belt require immediate resupply of critical commodities. Key corridors traverse damaged bridges, unstable slopes, and areas requiring security escort. Convoy movements must be consolidated onto cleared routes and timed to available force protection windows. Some commodities are bulky or heavy, constraining payloads per vehicle and requiring careful load planning.\n\nMission:\nDeliver priority life-sustaining supplies from depots to supported units within the current operational period while minimizing total operational cost. Cost elements include per-unit transport costs, shortage penalties for any unmet demand, per-vehicle deployment costs, and fixed route security risk costs that are incurred whenever a route is activated.\n\nExecution/Concept:\n- Source constraints: Each depot may issue only up to on-hand stock per commodity and period. No cross-loading between depots is assumed this period.\n- Demand fulfillment: Each supported unit has commodity-specific minimum requirements. Controlled shortfalls are permitted but incur penalties; these will be accepted only when the combined transport, vehicle, and route activation costs exceed the shortage penalty.\n- Vehicle capacity: Loads are governed by commodity volume/weight factors. The total loaded volume per depot-to-unit movement must not exceed vehicle capacity multiplied by the number of assigned vehicles.\n- Route activation and risk: Activating a route\u2014defined as dispatching any vehicles on a specific depot-to-unit corridor\u2014triggers a fixed Risk Cost representing escorts, route clearance, and quick-reaction force readiness. This fixed cost is charged once per activated route per period, regardless of how many vehicles traverse it. Additional per-unit transport costs still apply.\n- Fleet allocation: A limited fleet is available across all corridors. Vehicles may only operate on routes that have been activated. The aggregate number of deployed vehicles across all routes cannot exceed the fleet available this period.\n- Cost-minimizing approach: Prioritize flows that satisfy the highest-penalty commodities and destinations first, consolidate shipments onto the fewest feasible activated routes to amortize the fixed risk cost, and draw from the most cost-efficient depots without exceeding stock limits. Activate additional routes only when the marginal benefit of reduced shortages or lower transport costs outweighs the fixed risk and vehicle deployment costs.\n- Security and movement control: Movement Control will schedule convoys to align with engineer clearance and escort availability. Receiving units will provide offload teams and secure reception areas. If hazards or access degrade further, shipments will be re-routed to pre-identified alternates using the same activation cost logic.\n\nAnnex A - Logistics Data:\n{DEMANDS}\n{DEPOT_SUPPLIES}\n{TRANSPORT_COSTS}\n{RISK_COSTS}\n{PENALTY_COSTS}\n{SUPPLY_VOLUMES}\nFleet Parameters: Cost {VEHICLE_COST}, Capacity {VEHICLE_CAPACITY}, Total {TOTAL_VEHICLES}"
